# PsionNet -- Windows CE via DIRECT CONNECTION.
#
# THIS ROUTE DOES NOT WORK FOR NETWORKING. Kept for reference and so nobody
# repeats the investigation.
#
# A Direct Connection completes PPP perfectly: LCP, optional PAP, CCP, IPCP,
# addresses assigned, DNS accepted, and the device answers LCP echoes. Then it
# carries no traffic at all, and the device reports "you have been
# disconnected". Early on it also showed "No IPv4 adapter found" in ipconfig
# while PPP was fully established.
#
# This was tested to exhaustion: with and without PAP, with MS-CHAPv2 (the
# device NAKs it and demands PAP), with and without CCP, with and without
# MPPE, with WINS offered and refused, with Apple's ACSCP disabled, at 115200
# and at 19200, and finally with this file made byte-for-byte identical to
# peers/psion-ce-modem -- which works -- changing only the handshake. Same
# result every time.
#
# Conclusion: Microsoft's Direct Cable Connection is a DESKTOP PASSTHROUGH
# transport. It speaks PPP, then binds the link to the sync stack rather than
# to TCP/IP. The decision is made on the device after negotiation completes,
# so no server-side option can influence it.
#
# USE peers/psion-ce-modem INSTEAD: a dial-up connection through a modem entry
# is treated as a real network interface, and it works.

connect "/usr/sbin/chat -v -f /etc/ppp/psion-ce.chat"

# 19200, NOT 115200. CE's modem entry sets the line speed, and on the netBook
# Pro it defaults to 19200 -- reading at 115200 yields a stream of e0/00 noise
# rather than AT commands. If you can raise the speed in the device's modem
# settings, raise this to match; 19200 is only ~1.9 KB/s.
19200
10.0.2.1:10.0.2.2

ms-dns 1.1.1.1
ms-dns 9.9.9.9

noccp            # do not haggle over MPPE compression
noacsp           # Apple's proprietary extension; CE cannot understand it
noauth
local
nodetach
nodefaultroute
crtscts
asyncmap 0
mtu 1500
mru 1500
lcp-echo-interval 30
lcp-echo-failure 4
ipparam psionnet
debug
logfile /var/log/ppp-psion.log
